Use PostgreSQL's built-in full-text search. At about 38,000 rows growing by a few hundred a month, a separate search engine such as Elasticsearch would add infrastructure with no benefit.

Add a weighted `tsvector` column with a GIN index, so name matches rank above description matches:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search tsvector GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
) STORED;

CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING gin (search);
```

Then add a route in `routes/products.js`. It has to be defined before `/:id`, or Express will treat "search" as an id.

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = String(req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents
       FROM products
      WHERE active AND search @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
      ORDER BY ts_rank(search, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) DESC, name
      LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

`websearch_to_tsquery` accepts raw user input without throwing on stray punctuation, and the generated column keeps the index current without triggers.

One decision changes the design: is this search-as-you-type, or does the user submit a query? Full-text search matches whole words after stemming, so "wirel" will not find "wireless", and a typo will not match anything. If it is search-as-you-type or needs typo tolerance, add `pg_trgm` with a trigram index on `name` and fall back to it when the full-text query returns nothing. If it is submit-then-results, the sketch above is enough.

I left out the frontend, result paging, and relevance tuning.

